                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "It's amazing this came out originally in 1989. I remember watching my dad play this a long time ago. Since then, I've wanted to take a go at it and finally decided to.\n\nDragon Wars is a hard-core CRPG, but it's different from a lot of games of its time as it gives you a lot of latitude in exploration. Though you get bits and pieces of information giving you general direction, there is not the normal, \"Go here next,\" type of gameplay. You can explore and do things in many different orders. You can even skip some things altogether. Since I avoided walkthroughs for the most part, I no doubt wandered and backtracked way more than I needed to (over 80 hours of playtime for one playthrough).\n\nAt the start, you can use the pre-built party or build your own characters. I chose to use the pre-builds, but I would like to give it a go creating my own characters, especially now that I've finished the game. Playing through using very limited outside resources, I found I didn't assign skill points on leveling very efficiently.\n\nThe battle system is typical turn-based combat. You go up against groups of monsters, and like the original Bard's Tale series, the monsters can be at different distances from your party, allowing for strategy in ranged, melee, and magic usage throughout the battle.\n\nOne thing that makes the game unique for its time is the auto map feature. I ended up mapping things out in Excel anyway, just so I can mark things on the map since the auto map doesn't mark things. But a neat feature nonetheless.\n\nAs a fan of grid-based RPGs, I wholeheartedly recommend the game. There are few tedious things (bandaging each character individually comes to mind), but that's to be expected for a game that is over 35 years old. Overall, a great experience if you're willing to put in the time.",
